   "text": "Armstrong International at SVC Manufacturing (PepsiCo)\nTechnology Evaluation Review  ·  Sections 1–20\n1\n\nArmstrong / SVC\nSITE DESCRIPTION\nHost: SVC Manufacturing (PepsiCo*), Tolleson, AZ\nSub-sector: Soft drink manufacturing (NAICS 312111)\nProject: Vapor compression heat pump through process heat recovery at beverage manufacturing plant\n*Current Better Plants Partner\n\nFACILITY & OPERATING CONDITIONS\nProduction and co-packing arm for their popular sports drink brands, most notably Gatorade \nProcess consist of two heating steps (batching and pasteurization) and three cooling steps (trim cooling, bottle cooling, and divert cooling)\nExisting low-grade waste-heat streams with fuel-fired heating (natural gas)\nContinuous/near-continuous operation\nVENDOR DESCRIPTION\nName: Armstrong International, Three Rivers, MI\n\nEquipment: A water-sourced vapor compression heat pump system utilizing a low GWP refrigerant (R1234ze), combined with thermal energy storage tanks\n\nTechnology Classification: Early Commercial Technology / Waste Heat Recovery and Heat Pumps\n2\n\nArmstrong / SVC\nBASELINE TECHNOLOGY\nExisting partial heat exchange between (1 & 2) and (trim and bottle cooling) (~24% of heating load)\nKPI baseline: Fuel Use Ratio (2,000 Btu/gal), >5 yrs historical data\nEXISTING EQUIPMENT & PERFORMANCE\nSteam boilers and cooling towers\n\nNEW TECHNOLOGY\nVapor compression heat pump + thermal storage\nUpgrades low-grade heat to process-temperature heat\nTargets >70% heating load from recovery\nINSTALLATION SCOPE & PERFORMANCE\nEvaluate affected lines + thermal equipment only\nExclude unaffected lines from primary savings\nHx\n3\n\nArmstrong / SVC\nCLAIMED BENEFITS\nReduced natural gas use\nLower utility spend\nWater savings from thermal integration\n\nENERGY / COST / PRODUCTIVITY\nEnergy: reduced fuel intensity per gallon product\nCost: net utility savings (quantified in workbook notes)\nWater: reduction from integrated thermal management\nProductivity: stable if thermal delivery maintained\nTotal heat recovery increases from a baseline of 24% to 70%\nThermal storage from the divert cooler increases heat recovery from 24% to 40%\nHeat pumps utilizing bottle cooler waste heat increase heat recovery from 40% to over 70%\n\nThe Fuel Use Ratio drops from over 2,000 Btu/gal to under 1,000 Btu/gal on the four affected lines\nDirect heat recovery accounts for approximately 333 Btu/gal of the thermal energy savings\nHeat pumping accounts for the remaining 666 Btu/gal of the thermal energy savings\n\nThe project generates an estimated $500,000 per year in net utility savings\n\nCooling tower water consumption is reduced by approximately 16 million gallons per year\n\n4\n\nArmstrong / SVC\nM&V SCOPE & BOUNDARY\nEvaluate affected lines + thermal equipment only\nExclude unaffected lines from primary savings\nBoundaries: source loop → heat pump → storage → sink loop\n\nOBJECTIVES & APPROACH\nIPMVP Option B retrofit isolation\nKPIs: Fuel Use Ratio, NG reduction, utility cost\nNormalization: throughput, product mix, schedule\nEVALUATION KPIs\nUtility/fuel meters, electric submeters\nFlow + temperature sensors on source/sink loops\nProduction history (throughput, mix, schedule)\n\nKPI METRICS\nRegression/hybrid model for affected lines\nRoutine adjustments: throughput, product mix, schedule\nNon-routine: major line/process changes\nUncertainty quantification and QA/QC for missing intervals\n5\n\nArmstrong / SVC\nDATA COLLECTION\nUtility/fuel meters, electric submeters\nFlow + temperature sensors on source/sink loops\nProduction historian (throughput, mix, schedule)\nInterval: 15 min to hourly\n\nDATA ANALYSIS\nRegression/hybrid model for affected lines\nRoutine adjustments: throughput, product mix, schedule\n\nMODELING & NORMALIZATION\nNon-routine: major line/process changes\nUncertainty quantification and QA/QC for missing intervals\n6\n\nArmstrong / SVC\nCHALLENGES & CONCERNS\nNew meters at project start limit pre-period granularity\nAttribution complexity across multi-line plant\nRisk of confounding from production/maintenance changes (e.g., operating schedules, seasonal operating conditions, or maintenance activities or impacts) \n\nDATA / OPERATIONAL RISKS\nNew meters at project start limit pre-period granularity\nAttribution complexity across multi-line plant\nRisk of confounding from production/maintenance changes\nQUESTIONS FOR SITE & VENDOR\nWhat exact line boundaries and modes are in-scope?\nHow are storage dispatch rules defined?\nWhat fallback exists if heat pump availability drops?\nConfirm basis for >70% heating-load recovery target\nConfirm water calculation methodology\nAre any scheduled maintenance activities planned during the baseline or reporting period? \nRequired permits for the heat pump installation? \n7"
