   "text": "Technology Developer: Armstrong International\nHost Site: SVC Manufacturing, Inc. (PepsiCo)\nTitle: Vapor Compression Heat Pumping for Process Heat Recovery at Beverage Manufacturing Plant\nDescription: Increases the heat recovery from 24% of the heating load to >70% through heat pumps to increase quality of heat from cooling processes and thermal storage of heat pump outlet hot water\nHost Site Location: Tolleson, AZ\nIndustry Sector(s): Soft drink manufacturing (NAICS 312111)\nStage of Development: Early Commercial Technology – technology whose value and risks are understood by specialists for some applications, but the supply chain and/or full-scale production have not yet been fully established.\nPerformance Claim: Heat recovery improvements are seen in two parts:\nA) Thermal storage to capture heat from cooling step #2B (cooling of high temperature waste heat)\nB) Heat pumps to increase quality of heat from cooling step #2A (cooling of low temperature waste heat)\n\nVapor Compression Heat Pump and Thermal Storage at a Beverage Plant\nArmstrong International\n\nBaseline Technology\nBatching and pasteurization: heated through steam\nTrim, bottle and divert cooling: Cooled through water sprays; cold water provided by cooling tower\nCurrently, heat recovery (~24%) is done by integrating trim & bottle cooling with pasteurization\nNew Technology\nVapor compression heat pump + thermal storage\nUpgrades low-grade heat to process-temperature heat (~70% heat recovery)\nRecovered heat pre-heats pasteurization feed\n\nHeat Pump and Thermal Storage could recover >70% of Process Waste heat\nBaseline and New Technology Comparison\nArmstrong International\nBatching\nWater to 100°F\nPasteurization\nProduct to 205°F\nTrim cooling\n205°F to 185°F\nHot filling\n185°F\nTreated city water\nBottle cooler\n185°F to 100°F\nDivert cooler\n185°F to 100°F\nOverflow / bypass\nBottled product\nDiverted product\nBoiler steam\nNatural gas\nSteam\nExisting heat recovery (24%)\nTrim and bottle cooling waste-heat preheat beverage before pasteurization\nCooling towers\n85°F water supply\nRemaining heat goes\nto the cooling towers\nBatching\nWater to 100°F\nNew R2 preheater\nFor heat recovery\nPasteurization\nProduct to 205°F\nTrim cooling\n205°F to 185°F\nHot filling\n185°F\nTreated city water\nBottle cooler\n185°F to 100°F\nDivert cooler\n185°F to 100°F\nOverflow / bypass\nNew batch-water HX\nWarm tank loop heat\nReduced steam (Boiler)\nExisting trim heat\nrecovery retained\nHot tank\n170°F loop target\nHeat pumps\nCore technology\nWarm tank\nThermal buffer\n174°F design outlet\nElectricity\n170°F hot water\nReturn: ~110°F\nto warm tank\n110–120°F recovered heat\nDirect divert heat 170°F\nCooling towers\nUsed as backup\nNotes:\nTrim cooling is done from an existing heat recovery system, where the recovered heat is then sent to the steam. Trim cooling is needed because 205 in packaging may be too much heat\nHot filling and trim cooling is using excess heat currently. Pre-pasteurization is heated by currently recovered steam\n\nM&V Scope and Boundaries\n\nM&V includes electricity, boiler steam, and cooling tower water; Thermal storage capabilities will require quantification\nM&V\nArmstrong International\n\nKPI\nDefinition / units\nHeat captured /\ndelivered\nFrom warm and hot tanks, MMBtu\nRecovery share, (%)\nHeat-pump COP\nCondenser heat (th) /\nelectricity, (kWh/kWh)\nHX effectiveness\nActual / maximum\nheat transfer, (%)\nFuel intensity &\ndisplacement\nBtu/gal product\nSteam saved, (lb/yr)\nNG saved, (MMBtu/yr)\nElectricity\nHP, pumps, auxiliaries\nkWh, peak kW\nThermal storage\nUsable kWh(th)\nAvailability, (%)\nCooling-tower\nwater\nMakeup water reduced,\n(gal/yr)\nNet utility costs\nsavings\nFuel + water savings\nless power cost, ($/yr)\nPotential KPIs\n\nFour affected lines\n+ thermal equipment\nBottle cooling\n+ recovery HX\nDivert cooling\n+ recovery HX\nWarm tank\nHeat\npumps\nHot tank\nH\nH\nSource heat\nDirect heat\nH\nH\nHeat upgrade\nBatch-water\nHX\nExisting trim\nheat recovery\nR2 process\npreheater\nBatching + pasteurization\nFour affected production lines\nH\nH\nProduction volume (gal)\nElectricity\nE\nHP + pumps\nBoiler steam\nLine steam meter\nS\nCooling towers\nMakeup water meter\nW\nResidual heat / backup\nNotes:\nRecovery share (%): the percentage of the process’s total heating demand supplied by recovered heat. \n\nEight lines in the plant\n\nScenarios   Addressed baseline = listed pool × 10% / 25% / 50%. Savings = addressed baseline × performance factor.\nCentral case = 25%. Broad pool scenarios, not qualified-deployment forecasts. Addressed baseline and savings are different measures.\nEnergy Applicability\n370 TBtu/yr\n10 / 25 / 50%  implementation cases: 14 / 34 / 68 TBtu/yr\nBaseline  370 TBtu/yr selected process pool from applicable F&B, pulp & paper, alumina & automobile sector unit operations. Up to 37% energy reduction.\nApplies to  Dairy, beverages, meat/poultry, grain and oilseed hot water, sanitation, pasteurization, preheating, evaporation, bleaching and drying. \n\nWater\ncooling-tower makeup exposure\n2.16–2.70 M gal/yr\nBaseline per example facility with 1 million cooling ton-hours/yr. Not a national inventory.\nBaseline  EPA makeup benchmark: 2.16–2.70 gal/ton-hour at 3–6 concentration cycles. The facility cooling duty is an illustration.\nApplies to  Recoverable process-cooling loads paired with a simultaneous heat demand. Water benefit requires an actual reduction in evaporative tower duty.\nHP + thermal storage yields energy savings, but costs savings may not occur in every region\nSpaces for Opportunity\nArmstrong International\nMethod  370 × 10 / 25 / 50%. Savings already include the worksheet performance factors. Apply them only once."
