   "id": "joulesai-manus:doe-2026-09-29",
   "title": "ITV Initial Technology Assessment presented to DOE",
   "project": "joulesai-manus",
   "date": "2026-09-29",
   "source": "ITV Initial Tech Assessment Presentation to DOE 2026-09-29 .txt",
   "text": "Technology Developer: Joules AI Inc\nHost Site: Manus Bio Inc\nTitle: Reinforced learning-based autonomous control for total electricity cost reduction\nDescription: Deploys a reinforcement-learning (RL) control layer on top of the site's existing EMS/SCADA, battery energy storage system (BESS) controller, and utility interval-meter feeds.\nHost Site Location: Augusta, GA\nIndustry Sector(s): Bioindustrial and chemical manufacturing NAICS 325199\nStage of Development: Early commercial\nPerformance Claim: The primary performance benefit is a reduction in the total cost of electricity under Real-Time Pricing. Based on prior modeling and simulated evaluations using representative industrial load and pricing data, this approach is expected to increase the economic value captured from the BESS by approximately 10–20% beyond the savings achievable with incumbent rule-based control.\nAutonomous Control for Total Electricity Cost Reduction\nJoules AI\nGuidance:\nMost of this info can be pulled from the ITV Application Review Deck\nStage of Development can be pulled directly from application\n\nAdditional info:\nStage of Development: this project will validate the tech’s performance with specific feedstocks, and establishing operational data to support supply chain development.\n\nBaseline Technology\nRule-based or schedule-based battery dispatch (EMS logic)\nBaseline captures conventional BESS operation\nPerformance measured on total electricity cost and compliance\n\nNew Technology\nReinforcement-learning control platform for real-time dispatch\nNon-invasive integration via existing telemetry and control paths\nPhase 1: digital shadow → Phase 2: autonomous control\nM&V compares AI battery dispatch with rules based system\nBaseline and New Technology Comparison\nJoules AI\nMARKET\nGeorgia Power RTP\nHourly / day-ahead prices\n$0.06–$3.37/kWh observed\nSITE\nPlant demand\nFermentation + utilities\nProduction-state forecast\nASSET\nBESS telemetry\nSOC • power • alarms\nBMS / PCS / SCADA\nONSITE\nNormalizes telemetry\nChecks limits + rate constraints\nSecure cloud–OT mediation\nCLOUD\nJoulesOS intelligence\nDigital twin + forecasts\nRL policy optimization\nScenario and M&V analytics\nFINAL AUTHORITY\nBESS supervisory control\nAccepts, constrains, or rejects setpoints\nBMS / PCS protections remain authoritative\nPHASE 1 · DIGITAL SHADOW\nRead-only: compare baseline dispatch with simulated AI decisions\nPHASE 2 · AUTONOMOUS\nValidated setpoints flow through the edge; local override is retained\nOUTCOME\nLower TCE • Better price response • Constraint compliance\nJoulesAI Edge\nMARKET\nGeorgia Power RTP\nHourly / day-ahead prices\n$0.06–$3.37/kWh observed\nSITE\nPlant demand\nASSET\nBESS telemetry\nSOC • Power • Alarms\nBMS / PCS / SCADA\nRules based control\nNormalizes telemetry\n\nChecks limits + rate constraints\n\nSecure cloud–OT mediation\n\nFINAL AUTHORITY\nBESS supervisory control\nAccepts, constrains, or rejects setpoints\nBMS / PCS protections remain authoritative\nOUTCOME\nLower TCE • Constraint compliance\nTCE: Total cost of electricity, BESS: Battery energy storage system, BMS: battery management system, PCS: Power conversion system, OT: Operational technology, SCADA: Supervisory control and data acquisition, M&V: Measurement and verification \nGuidance: \nFor visuals, use block diagrams rather than full detail graphics\n\nM&V Scope and Boundaries\n\nPotential KPIs\n\nJoules AI orchestration cuts electricity costs 10–20% while maintaining facility resilience—capturing value that static rules miss during extreme price volatility\nM&V\nJoules AI\nTotal Cost of Electricity ($/MWh delivered) vs. rule-based baseline\nPeak demand reduction (kW) and demand-charge savings ($/cycle)\nLoad-shift energy (kWh moved from high- to low-price intervals)\nEquivalent full battery cycles per year \nBESS RTE (measured) discharged energy/charged energy\nForecast accuracy: price MAPE and load MAPE at 15-min and 1-hr horizons\nConstraint-violation rate (% of intervals) — SOC, ramp, production\nMEASUREMENT BOUNDARY\nBESS dispatch decision + resulting grid exchange\nOUTSIDE\nObserved drivers\nFacility load + production state\n\nRTP price signal + forecasts ( Same interval condition )\nCOUNTERFACTUAL\nBaseline path\nRule-based dispatch\nActual operation or replay\nJoulesAI RL \nJoulesAI path\nDigital Shadow simulation\nor autonomous dispatch\nCONTROLLED\nSame BESS constraints\nSOC bounds • ramp rates\nPower limits • fallback rules\nMEASURED\nTime-aligned evidence\nGrid import + utility bill\nBESS logs and throughput\nConstraint / alarm records\nSAVINGS\nBaseline TCE − JoulesAI TCE\nINSIDE\nDispatch logic, BESS response, grid exchange, TCE, cycling, and constraint compliance\nOUTSIDE / NORMALIZE\nProduction-driven load changes, seasonality, outages, tariff changes, and non-routine events\nPreferred evaluation: interval-level Digital Shadow counterfactual, supported by historical and pre/post checks\nKPI\nDefinition / units\nTotal Cost of Electricity\n$/kWh+Demand charges, using RTP in both cases\nPeak demand reduction and demand charge savings\nkW, $\nShifted energy\nkWh moved from high to low price signals\nBESS RTE\nDischarged energy/charged energy\nConstraint-violation rate\n% intervals the SOC, ramp-rate, production related violations, charge/discharge cycles\nGuidance:\nUse a graphic to present the M&V scope and boundary. It’s also helpful to show what ancillary equipment is involved.\nLimit KPIs to quantifiable only\nApproach: \nHigh-level overview of M&V plan/analysis\nAssumption is all (if not most) projects will be using IPMVP option B. Tech leads to verify; note IPMVP approach only if not.\n\nScenarios   Addressed electricity = listed pool × 10% / 25% / 50%. Conditional bill savings = selected bill × 15%.\nCentral case = 25%. Broad scenarios, not deployment forecasts. The 15% simulation benchmark concerns bills, not physical energy savings.\nCost\nconditional electricity-bill savings\n$1.49 B/yr saved\n25% implementation. 10 / 25 / 50% cases: $0.60 / 1.49 / 2.98 B/yr conditional savings.\nBaseline  $0.10/kWh average bill valuation. Apply the 15% airport-simulation benchmark to the selected bill proxy. No industrial field validation yet in the supplied evidence. [1,2]\nApplies to  Sites with suitable storage and time-varying prices or demand charges. A flat tariff without demand charges gives 0% assumed arbitrage benefit.\nMethod  At 25%: $9.92 B/yr proxy bill × 15% = $1.49 B/yr. Actual residual grid bills must replace this proxy. Excludes software/gainshare fees, battery capital and added wear.\nJoules AI / Manus Bio: battery dispatch optimization\nSources: [1] Supplied workbook/application  [2] JoulesAI airport simulation  [3] Energies (2024). Source links and calculations in notes.\nSpaces for Opportunity\nJoules AI\nEnergy\nelectricity baseline addressed\n396.9 TWh/yr\n25% implementation. 10 / 25 / 50% cases: 135.44 / 338.60 / 677.21 TBtu/yr addressed.\nBaseline  1,354.42 TBtu/yr (396.9 TWh/yr) across 409 electrical end-use rows in 34 mapped sector groups. [1]\nApplies to  Site-level dispatch in chemicals/biotech, refining, food/beverage, pulp/paper/wood, metals, cement/lime, glass and automotive manufacturing.\n\nMethod  Pool × implementation share. Requires battery storage or genuinely controllable assets and a suitable tariff. The selected electricity is not all routed through the battery."
