   "source": "ITV Initial Tech Assessment Presentation to DOE 2026-09-29 .txt",
   "text": "Technology Developer: CEIA USA, Ltd.\nHost Site: Donaldson Company\nTitle: Induction-heated hot plates for curing a liquid adhesive sealant in an air filter assembly\nDescription: Induction-based hot plates employ alternating electromagnetic field to induce eddy current. The plate transfers heat to filter end-cap/adhesive assembly through conduction, eventually curing them\nHost Site Location: Greeneville, TN\nIndustry Sector(s): Industrial filtration and discrete manufacturing (NAICS 333411)\nStage of Development: Underutilized – technology is commercialized, but adoption within the U.S. or within a relevant industry is not widespread.\nPerformance Claim: Claimed improvements are shown below:\nThe induction heating technology is expected to use 50% less energy than the current process, lowering overall operating expenses and heat load on the factory floor\nFaster warm-up times and less waste heat to the surrounding environment.\nInduction-Heated Hot Plates for Curing a Liquid Adhesive Sealant in an Air Filter Assembly\nCEIA USA, Ltd.\n\nBaseline Technology\nResistance elements heat aluminum curing plates.\nSlow warm-up often keeps heaters on during pauses. \nPlate heat cures adhesive through the metal end cap.\nNew Technology\nInduction heats a thinner steel plate beneath the filter.\nFaster warm-up enables shutdowns during pauses.\nIR temperature feedback controls plate heating.\n\nInduction-heated plates could cut curing energy use by 50%\nBaseline and New Technology Comparison\nCEIA USA, Ltd.\n\nDispense + assemble\nSealant, end cap and filter\nStage + shuttle\nLoad curing plate\nCure adhesive sealant\nHeat through the end cap\nTime / temperature per spec.\nConveyor transfer\nTo second potting station\nRepeat at opposite end\nDispense, assemble and cure\nSlow warm-up\nHeaters often stay on\nduring production pauses.\nHeat losses to\nsurrounding air\nAluminum hot plate\nConducts heat to end cap\nResistance heater\nMated beneath plate\nConduction\nHeat by\nconduction\nElectricity\nDispense + assemble\nSealant, end cap and filter\nStage + shuttle\nLoad curing plate\nCure adhesive sealant\nHeat through the end cap\nTime / temperature per spec.\nConveyor transfer\nTo second potting station\nRepeat at opposite end\nDispense, assemble and cure\nIR pyrometer\nPlate temperature\nThin steel hot plate\nHeat forms within the plate\nInduction coil\n+ remote heating head\nWater-cooled\nCEIA generator\n12 kW + controller\n30–60 kHz\nHeat by\nconduction\nMagnetic\nfield\nAC power\nTemperature\nfeedback\nAir Cooled chiller\nCooling\nElectricity\n\nM&V includes electricity utilities; Seal quality, chiller make-up water and ramp-up times are of interest\nM&V\nCEIA USA, Ltd.\nElectricity\n2 hot plates at Line 13 (10 hot plates run on incumbent)\nE\nTotal kWh + kW\nHeaters + controls\nResistance baseline /\ninduction retrofit\nAir Cooled Chiller + pump\nCoolant loop\nPlate heat\nHot plate + filter\nAdhesive cure\nPlate temperature + cure time\nStartup, idle, curing\nLot / plate / product ID\nQuality + production records\nSeal-quality checks\nAccepted / failed / reworked\nProduct counts\nEarned-hour records\nMaintenance logs\nKPI\nDefinition / units\nStation energy\nkWh including chiller\nand controls\nEnergy / cure\nkWh / cure event\nEnergy / filter\nkWh / accepted filter\nEnergy / earned\nhour\nkWh / earned hour\nWarm-up time\nMinutes to cure-ready\nplate temperature\nIdle / peak power\nIdle kW, peak kW\nDefined meter interval\nFailure Rate\nCure failures (#, %)\nRework rate (%)\nRadiant heating\nEnergy effects of radiant heating reduction\nM&V Scope and Boundaries\n\nPotential KPIs\n\nNotes:\nEarned hours= Quantity produced * Standard hours per unit\n\nScenarios   Addressed baseline = listed pool × 10% / 25% / 50%. Savings = addressed baseline × performance factor.\nCentral case = 25%. Broad pool scenarios, not qualified-deployment forecasts. Addressed baseline and savings are different measures.\nEnergy Applicability\ncuring/bonding baseline addressed\n 3 TBtu/yr \n25% implementation. 10 / 25 / 50% cases: 0.30 / 0.74 / 1.48 TBtu/yr addressed.\nBaseline  3 TBtu/yr mapped vehicle-curing fuel proxy. Census reports 479 broader fan/blower/air-purification establishments (2023).\nApplies to  Filter adhesives, appliance gaskets, motor magnets/resins, vehicle sealants, polymer/composite bonding and medical-device assembly.\n\nEnergy Applicability\npackage-sealing baseline addressed\n5 TBtu/yr\n25% implementation. 10 / 25 / 50% cases: 0.50 / 1.24 / 2.48 TBtu/yr addressed.\nBaseline  5 TBtu/yr mapped electricity for milk, brewery and distillery packaging. This includes non-heating duties. \nApplies to  Compatible foil/container closures in food, dairy, beverages, pharma, cosmetics and chemicals. Coils and packages differ from host heated-plate curing. \n\nCEIA / Donaldson: induction heating\nSpaces for Opportunity\nCEIA USA, Ltd.\nMethod  Vehicle pool × 10 / 25 / 50%. Core filter curing is not in this energy map. The 479 count includes non-filter plants.\u000bMethod  Packaging pool × 10 / 25 / 50%. Addressed baseline is not energy saved. Cost cases assume 40% net electricity savings."
